+### Font utilities
+
+The font keys ship whole in the utilities layer from the `src/styles/utilities/_font.scss`
+partial: the `.font-monospace` class, the `.fs-1` to `.fs-6` size classes, the `.fst-italic` and
+`.fst-normal` style classes, the `.fw-lighter` to `.fw-bolder` weight classes, and the `.lh-1`,
+`.lh-sm`, `.lh-base`, and `.lh-lg` line-height classes. The release writes none of these entries
+responsive, so each class ships at the empty infix alone and no font class writes a breakpoint
+infix.
+
+A size class resolves what the heading class of the same level resolves: the `.fs-1` class reads
+the `--vn-size-8` token the `.h1` class reads, down to the `.fs-6` class, which reads the
+`--vn-size-3` token the `.h6` class reads. So a retuned size token moves a size class and its
+heading class together, and a size class holds one size at every viewport. The release scales its
+first four sizes with the viewport and caps them from a 1200px viewport; Veneer writes neither the
+fluid formula nor the cap, as the heading classes write neither.
+
+The `.lh-base` class reads the `--vn-line-body` token the body text reads, so a retuned body line
+moves the class with the body text. The `.font-monospace` class reads the `--bs-font-monospace`
+alias, as the release writes it, and the `_tokens.scss` partial declares that alias over the
+`--vn-font-mono-base` token, so a root retune of either name moves the class. The weights, the
+styles, and the other line heights stay literal, because no published Veneer token carries them.
+The `.fw-lighter` and `.fw-bolder` classes resolve against the weight their parent resolves, as the
+release's relative keywords do. No font class reads the density factor or the color mode.
+
+The partial writes every entry through the `utility` mixin that § Styles describes, in the
+release's map order: the family, size, style, weight, and line-height entries. No two entries set
+one property, so the order that decides a resolution is the order inside an entry: an element
+carrying the `.fw-bold` and `.fw-light` classes resolves the `700` weight, because the map writes
+the bold step after the light step. Every declaration carries the `!important` flag the release
+writes. The classes sit in the utilities layer, so a size class beats a heading or display class
+on the same element: an element carrying the `.h1` and `.fs-6` classes resolves the size the
+`--vn-size-3` token carries. § Styles shows the escape from an important utility inside the
+utilities layer.
+
+The `tests/src/styles/utilities/font.test.ts` proof reads each size class against its heading
+class at the 390 and 1280 viewports and under its retuned size token, the base line height under a
+retuned body line, the monospace class under a retuned alias and a retuned token, each weight under
+two parent weights, each style inside an oblique parent, each line height over a fixed font size,
+the absence of an infixed class, a dark island and the density factor, the order inside an entry,
+the size class over a heading class, the priority over a later unlayered rule, and the escape
+inside the utilities layer.
+
+These are the keys' recorded departures.
+
+- **The size classes read Veneer's heading scale.** The release writes the `.fs-1` to `.fs-4`
+  classes as a fluid `calc()` expression of a rem length and a viewport share, capped at a literal
+  size from a 1200px viewport, and the `.fs-5` and `.fs-6` classes as literal sizes. Veneer writes
+  the `var(--vn-size-8)` to `var(--vn-size-3)` values and no cap, so each class resolves its
+  heading class's size at every viewport.
+- **The base line height reads Veneer's body line.** The release writes the `1.5` factor; Veneer
+  writes the `var(--vn-line-body)` value, which resolves to the same factor and moves with the body
+  text.
+
